The \Encoding{} subnet encodes the history of a target word into a dense vector (known as \textit{context} or \textit{history} representation). We may either leverage FFNNs \cite{NLM} or RNNs \cite{RNNLM} as the \Encoding{} subnet, but RNNs typically yield a better performance \cite{Sundermeyer:2015gj}.


The \Prediction{} subnet outputs a distribution of target words as
\begin{align}
p(w = w_i|h) = &\dfrac{\exp(s(h, w_i))}
{\sum_j \exp(s(h, w_j))}, \label{eq:sNNLM}\\
s(h, w_i) = & \trans{\vector{W}_i} \vector{h} + b_i, \label{eq:sNNLM:score}
\end{align}
where $\vector{h}$ is the vector representation of context/history $h$, obtained by the
\Encoding{} subnet.

